<br/>

>The following are alternative approaches to this PR:

<details>
<summary><b>1. Retain one monolithic package</b></summary>

<dl>
<dd>

- ➕ Simpler release ordering and dependency management
- ➕ Avoids coordinating versions across five packages
- ➖ Forces browser consumers to receive Node-only capture and storage concerns
- ➖ Prevents independent reuse of generation and presentation contracts
- ➖ Creates a larger compatibility and bundle surface

</dd>
</dl>

</details>

<details>
<summary><b>2. Split publishing and hardening into separate PRs</b></summary>

<dl>
<dd>

- ➕ Reduces individual review scope and regression isolation
- ➕ Allows public package boundaries to stabilize before behavioral changes
- ➖ Publishing would expose capture and storage APIs before required safety guarantees
- ➖ Requires temporary compatibility code and repeated release validation

</dd>
</dl>

</details>

>**Recommendation:** Keep the layered package split and release validation used by this PR: it establishes clear browser/Node boundaries and tests the actual external-consumer experience. A monolith would undermine reuse, while splitting the hardening from publication would risk releasing insufficiently bounded public APIs; reviewers should nevertheless examine package contracts, Git safety, storage migration/recovery, and release automation as distinct passes.

</dd>
</dl>

</details>

